# Whether an interval with 2-limb bounds is the whole line (milestone2.md, "One enclosure
# interface", the row of is_entire()): the leading limbs of the bounds are -inf and +inf. A
# canonical bound with an infinite leading limb is that infinity. Exact; false for the empty set.
# For every precision.
kernel:        interval_entire_2
input:         xl = xl0 xl1
input:         xu = xu0 xu1
output:        mask m
requires:      interval xl xu
ensures:       certain m entire x x=xl,xu exact
min_precision: 1
source:        the leading limbs of the bounds compared with the infinities (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        unverified
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   unverified
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the result is a mask, which has no non-finite value, and a comparison with NaN is false; certain states what comes out for every canonical operand
unused:        xl1 a canonical bound is infinite exactly when its leading limb is
unused:        xu1 a canonical bound is infinite exactly when its leading limb is

mi = neg(const inf)
a  = eq(xl0, mi)
b  = eq(xu0, const inf)
m  = and(a, b)
